From: G81EM1 bring-up <g81em1@localhost>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 08:40:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] surfaceflinger: ignore invalid zero HWC VSYNC timestamps

The legacy MT6580 HWC2 shim reports timestamp zero on every VSYNC callback.
Passing those samples into DispSync produces a zero period and a SIGFPE in
DispSync::updateModelLocked(). Ignore the invalid hardware samples so the
software timing model remains authoritative.
---
 .../surfaceflinger/DisplayHardware/HWComposer.cpp | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/DisplayHardware/HWComposer.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/DisplayHardware/HWComposer.cpp
index 3f3c67b1e..3e1198a76 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/DisplayHardware/HWComposer.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/DisplayHardware/HWComposer.cpp
@@ -170,6 +170,16 @@ bool HWComposer::onVsync(hwc2_display_t displayId, int64_t timestamp,
     {
         Mutex::Autolock _l(mLock);
 
+        // This legacy MTK HWC2 implementation reports zero for every VSYNC
+        // timestamp. Feeding those samples to DispSync makes its measured
+        // period zero and eventually triggers an integer divide-by-zero.
+        // Ignore invalid hardware samples and let DispSync use its software
+        // timing model instead. Do not log here: the callback runs every
+        // display period and would flood logd.
+        if (timestamp <= 0) {
+            return false;
+        }
+
         // There have been reports of HWCs that signal several vsync events
         // with the same timestamp when turning the display off and on. This
         // is a bug in the HWC implementation, but filter the extra events
